Thank you.

00:01:01.274 --> 00:01:04.574
And finally, if you have missed

00:01:04.574 --> 00:01:07.194
some other "Ed Talks" and "Homerooms,"

00:01:07.194 --> 00:01:11.304
you can find us, audio
versions of those talks,

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:13.894
on your favorite podcast provider

00:01:13.894 --> 00:01:17.074
at looking for "Homeroom with Sal,"

00:01:17.074 --> 00:01:20.814
and you can listen to Sal
and I interview other folks

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:24.277
who are influencers in
the education world.

00:01:26.544 --> 00:01:30.014
And with that, let me welcome Fenesha.

00:01:30.014 --> 00:01:32.384
Fenesha, welcome, thanks for joining us.

00:01:32.384 --> 00:01:35.374
- Hi, Kristen, thank you
so much for having me.

00:01:35.374 --> 00:01:36.534
- Absolutely.

00:01:36.534 --> 00:01:40.744
So first, let me just give
a quick background for folks

00:01:40.744 --> 00:01:43.974
on who is NWEA?

00:01:43.974 --> 00:01:48.354
So NWEA, as I said, is one
of our partner organizations

00:01:48.354 --> 00:01:51.824
and they're a nonprofit that
supports students and educators

00:01:53.904 --> 00:01:55.764
in more than 146 countries

00:01:55.764 --> 00:01:58.934
doing research, assessment solutions,

00:01:58.934 --> 00:02:01.664
policy and advocacy services,
professional learning,

00:02:01.664 --> 00:02:03.624
and school improvement services.

00:02:03.624 --> 00:02:06.464
And I know that that core to NWEA's work

00:02:06.464 --> 00:02:08.974
is really fighting for equity driving,

00:02:08.974 --> 00:02:13.414
impacting classrooms, and
pushing for systemic change

00:02:13.414 --> 00:02:14.934
in our education communities.

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:19.654
So, that is a big bunch
of work to bite off.

00:02:19.654 --> 00:02:21.257
Tell me what your role is.

00:02:22.754 --> 00:02:25.524
- Kristen, I love being at NWEA.

00:02:25.524 --> 00:02:27.574
I've been at NWEA for about 10 years

00:02:27.574 --> 00:02:31.974
and what I do is really support
our educators nationwide.

00:02:31.974 --> 00:02:35.364
And I do that by designing
the professional learning

00:02:35.364 --> 00:02:38.364
that teachers need to stay
on top of their craft.

00:02:38.364 --> 00:02:40.214
I really enjoy the work.

00:02:40.214 --> 00:02:41.134
- That is awesome.

00:02:41.134 --> 00:02:42.274
How did you get into that?

00:02:42.274 --> 00:02:45.387
How did you get to be in this
role of supporting teachers?

00:02:46.284 --> 00:02:48.264
- I think to support teachers in this way,

00:02:48.264 --> 00:02:49.644
you had to have been a teacher,

00:02:49.644 --> 00:02:50.924
so that's how I started my career.

00:02:50.924 --> 00:02:53.764
I started teaching middle school math

00:02:53.764 --> 00:02:55.384
in a very large school district,

00:02:55.384 --> 00:02:58.174
and it was through an
alternative certification program

00:02:58.174 --> 00:02:59.574
for career changers.

00:02:59.574 --> 00:03:01.894
So these were people who
had established careers,

00:03:01.894 --> 00:03:02.754
and they said, "You know what?

00:03:02.754 --> 00:03:04.374
I really wanna support students."

00:03:04.374 --> 00:03:06.414
And so I started off doing that

00:03:06.414 --> 00:03:09.584
and then was very quickly immersed

00:03:09.584 --> 00:03:11.064
into the world of professional learning.

00:03:11.064 --> 00:03:13.844
And so I started designing
learning for teachers,

00:03:13.844 --> 00:03:15.504
coaching teachers in math,

00:03:15.504 --> 00:03:17.304
and that led me to where I am today.

00:03:18.334 --> 00:03:19.734
- That is awesome.

00:03:19.734 --> 00:03:23.654
So, I mentioned that one of
the things that NWEA does

00:03:23.654 --> 00:03:28.324
is research and understanding
more about student learning,

00:03:28.324 --> 00:03:30.804
and I know you've just released a report

00:03:30.804 --> 00:03:32.544
on learning during COVID.

00:03:32.544 --> 00:03:35.344
What were the kinds of questions
you were investigating?

00:03:36.664 --> 00:03:37.774
- Yeah, great question.

00:03:37.774 --> 00:03:40.074
Our research team, Kristen,
really wanted to see

00:03:40.074 --> 00:03:42.694
how students were fairing
during the pandemic.

00:03:42.694 --> 00:03:44.084
That was our main focus.

00:03:44.084 --> 00:03:47.744
And what we noticed is that
if we focus on the good,

00:03:47.744 --> 00:03:49.404
gains were made, right?

00:03:49.404 --> 00:03:51.064
These were a very challenging two years,

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:52.434
but gains were made.

00:03:52.434 --> 00:03:55.554
However, students showed
a lower performance

00:03:55.554 --> 00:03:57.514
compared to the past trends.

00:03:57.514 --> 00:03:59.414
And when we think about math education,

00:03:59.414 --> 00:04:00.984
there were declines in math

00:04:00.984 --> 00:04:03.664
across all the grades and all the groups.

00:04:03.664 --> 00:04:04.847
So we think about,

00:04:05.744 --> 00:04:07.914
but you have a nice visual there,

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:10.904
the largest declines in math, Kristen,

00:04:10.904 --> 00:04:13.864
were among students of color

00:04:13.864 --> 00:04:16.882
and students in high poverty schools.

00:04:16.882 --> 00:04:18.124
And so I think one of the biggest things

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:20.294
we should do right now
is probably talk about,

00:04:20.294 --> 00:04:21.817
what are we gonna do about it?

00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:24.984
- Absolutely.

00:04:24.984 --> 00:04:26.404
I liked the positive bit

00:04:26.404 --> 00:04:31.404
that people did learn
something over the pandemic,

00:04:31.624 --> 00:04:33.524
but didn't get as far

00:04:34.377 --> 00:04:36.334
as we would have liked them to get likely.

00:04:36.334 --> 00:04:38.314
So, let's start with parents

00:04:38.314 --> 00:04:40.014
and thinking about parents first.

00:04:40.014 --> 00:04:42.987
What can parents do to support
students in learning math?

00:04:44.794 --> 00:04:46.514
- That is the question,

00:04:46.514 --> 00:04:48.284
particularly because so
many of our young people

00:04:48.284 --> 00:04:50.674
spent good quality time with their parents

00:04:50.674 --> 00:04:51.574
these past couple years.

00:04:51.574 --> 00:04:54.244
So I know that question
was coming up repeatedly.

00:04:54.244 --> 00:04:56.124
As a parent, how can I support my child?

00:04:56.124 --> 00:04:57.534
What do I need to do?

00:04:57.534 --> 00:04:59.964
I think, Kristen, what we
really wanna encourage parents

00:04:59.964 --> 00:05:03.174
to do is get their students
talking about math.

00:05:03.174 --> 00:05:04.124
We wanna help young people

00:05:04.124 --> 00:05:06.574
get their thoughts out of their heads

00:05:06.574 --> 00:05:08.164
and let it come out and come through

00:05:08.164 --> 00:05:10.427
in the best way that students can do that.

00:05:11.874 --> 00:05:15.654
I think we can actually learn
a lot from our young people

00:05:15.654 --> 00:05:18.794
in the sense that students
always ask, why, "Why?

00:05:18.794 --> 00:05:19.627
Why does this work?

00:05:19.627 --> 00:05:21.104
Why do I do it that way?"

00:05:21.104 --> 00:05:24.254
I think as parents, we
need to do that same thing.

00:05:24.254 --> 00:05:27.094
Start asking why a lot
more of our students,

00:05:27.094 --> 00:05:29.051
particularly when they come
to us and they might say,

00:05:29.051 --> 00:05:30.664
"Hey, can you help me work
through this problem?"

00:05:30.664 --> 00:05:32.804
Or, "I don't understand."

00:05:32.804 --> 00:05:33.864
Throw that question out there.

00:05:33.864 --> 00:05:35.734
Well, why would you do this?

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:36.844
And why does this work?

00:05:36.844 --> 00:05:38.214
And why do you think that?

00:05:38.214 --> 00:05:40.414
And I think that helps
us to really get insight

00:05:40.414 --> 00:05:42.111
into how students are thinking about math

00:05:42.111 --> 00:05:44.011
and the ways that they think about it.

00:05:45.384 --> 00:05:46.964
- That makes sense.

00:05:46.964 --> 00:05:50.004
I've heard friends who are parents

00:05:50.004 --> 00:05:52.984
worry about what do they do
if they don't know the answer,

00:05:52.984 --> 00:05:56.464
if they don't know how
to do a math problem

00:05:56.464 --> 00:05:58.164
that their child brings to them.

00:05:58.164 --> 00:05:59.597
Any suggestions for that?

00:06:00.534 --> 00:06:03.272
- I think Khan Academy
is a great suggestion.

00:06:03.272 --> 00:06:04.105
(Kristen laughing)

00:06:04.105 --> 00:06:06.485
- I wasn't trying to lead you there.

00:06:06.485 --> 00:06:08.762
(both women laughing)

00:06:08.762 --> 00:06:11.594
- I think before we start
seeking external resources,

00:06:11.594 --> 00:06:13.814
like Khan Academy is a
great external resource,

00:06:13.814 --> 00:06:17.434
but I think really having the students

00:06:17.434 --> 00:06:20.714
step into the position
of an empowered learner

00:06:20.714 --> 00:06:22.804
with their parents is going to be key.

00:06:22.804 --> 00:06:25.654
So empowered learner,
meaning, you know what,

00:06:25.654 --> 00:06:27.104
I do bring something to the table.

00:06:27.104 --> 00:06:29.844
I do have something that I
understand about the content,

00:06:29.844 --> 00:06:31.274
but I also have a stumbling block.

00:06:31.274 --> 00:06:32.894
And so if I can come to the table

00:06:32.894 --> 00:06:36.834
and start talking through my
misconceptions as a parent,

00:06:36.834 --> 00:06:39.214
what you can do is just
help guide my thinking,

00:06:39.214 --> 00:06:40.454
help pull it out of me.

00:06:40.454 --> 00:06:42.554
You don't need to know all
of the content to do that.

00:06:42.554 --> 00:06:44.147
You just need to know how
to ask really good questions

00:06:44.147 --> 00:06:46.594
and how to read me as your child

00:06:46.594 --> 00:06:48.524
and understand my body language

00:06:48.524 --> 00:06:50.021
and the things that I
might be stumbling on

00:06:50.021 --> 00:06:52.021
and the things that I'm confident about.

00:06:54.224 --> 00:06:56.664
- That makes sense, and I
know there's good research

00:06:56.664 --> 00:06:59.611
to support that using the language of math

00:06:59.611 --> 00:07:03.954
and just talking about math
as being really important

00:07:03.954 --> 00:07:06.354
in learning outcomes, so
that totally makes sense.

00:07:06.354 --> 00:07:09.014
It's about having the
conversation sometimes

00:07:09.014 --> 00:07:13.094
instead of having to find
the right answer right away.

00:07:13.094 --> 00:07:14.857
That rings true.
- That's right.

00:07:15.924 --> 00:07:18.804
- All right, so what about teachers?

00:07:18.804 --> 00:07:22.284
We've gotten this idea of
these maybe learning gaps

00:07:22.284 --> 00:07:25.994
that have opened up where students

00:07:25.994 --> 00:07:28.884
aren't where they might
have been in the past

00:07:28.884 --> 00:07:30.424
coming into the new year.

00:07:30.424 --> 00:07:31.834
What should teachers be thinking about

00:07:31.834 --> 00:07:33.057
to address some of that?

00:07:35.074 --> 00:07:36.784
- Hmm, that's a really good question, too.

00:07:36.784 --> 00:07:40.864
I think, one, we should
all come to an agreement

00:07:40.864 --> 00:07:43.074
that there was a lot
of interrupted learning

00:07:43.074 --> 00:07:45.524
that took place, and I
think once people are okay

00:07:45.524 --> 00:07:47.014
with admitting that, you know what,

00:07:47.014 --> 00:07:49.704
there were some breaks in the
routines and how we learned.

00:07:49.704 --> 00:07:53.254
And because of that interrupted learning,

00:07:53.254 --> 00:07:55.634
students might have some
unfinished learning,

00:07:55.634 --> 00:07:58.644
and the unfinished learning
are like the skills

00:07:58.644 --> 00:08:01.294
and the prerequisite knowledge
that students might need

00:08:01.294 --> 00:08:03.934
in order to be ready to
learn what's coming next.

00:08:03.934 --> 00:08:07.984
And so I think teachers are
well-positioned right now

00:08:07.984 --> 00:08:11.744
to really assess what unfinished learning

00:08:11.744 --> 00:08:13.474
students might have.

00:08:13.474 --> 00:08:17.164
And they can do that through
the use of assessments.

00:08:17.164 --> 00:08:20.434
The data can tell us what
students might be ready to learn,

00:08:20.434 --> 00:08:23.444
what they might have already mastered,

00:08:23.444 --> 00:08:26.234
and we can do that through actual tests,

00:08:26.234 --> 00:08:28.254
and we can also do that
through formative assessment,

00:08:28.254 --> 00:08:30.974
which is something that teachers
do so skillfully every day.

00:08:30.974 --> 00:08:33.774
And that's informal ways to get data,

00:08:33.774 --> 00:08:36.807
to inform instruction
right on the spot realtime.

00:08:38.214 --> 00:08:39.944
- What are some of your favorites

00:08:39.944 --> 00:08:41.764
of those kind of more informal things

00:08:41.764 --> 00:08:44.374
that teachers do to start to understand

00:08:44.374 --> 00:08:46.497
where students are, what their skills are?

00:08:47.334 --> 00:08:48.924
- Ooh, that's a great question.

00:08:48.924 --> 00:08:51.364
I love math language routines,

00:08:51.364 --> 00:08:54.874
and so I think about a
math language routine

00:08:54.874 --> 00:08:57.554
of the problem with
three reads, for example.

00:08:57.554 --> 00:08:59.824
So, we're working on a math problem.

00:08:59.824 --> 00:09:00.794
I'm simply gonna start off

00:09:00.794 --> 00:09:02.624
by asking you to read the problem

00:09:02.624 --> 00:09:03.651
and then I'm gonna ask you,

00:09:03.651 --> 00:09:05.604
"What is this problem asking us about?"

00:09:05.604 --> 00:09:07.374
So in simply asking that question,

00:09:07.374 --> 00:09:08.654
I'm getting an understanding

00:09:08.654 --> 00:09:10.674
of how my students are interpreting that.

00:09:10.674 --> 00:09:13.284
And then we might read the question again,

00:09:13.284 --> 00:09:17.324
and then I'll say, "Okay, well,
what information do we need

00:09:17.324 --> 00:09:18.604
in order to solve this problem?"

00:09:18.604 --> 00:09:19.894
So that gives me insight

00:09:19.894 --> 00:09:22.234
into the student's level of readiness

00:09:22.234 --> 00:09:26.514
in terms of background
knowledge that they might need.

00:09:26.514 --> 00:09:29.144
And so it's really helping
me to unpack the context

00:09:29.144 --> 00:09:32.264
and the purpose from my
students' understanding.

00:09:32.264 --> 00:09:34.944
Teachers are generally very well skilled

00:09:34.944 --> 00:09:36.744
at the strategies that we might use.

00:09:36.744 --> 00:09:39.194
Another thing we might do are
entrance and exit tickets.

00:09:39.194 --> 00:09:40.804
- Wait, I think you said three reads.

00:09:40.804 --> 00:09:41.774
Is there a third read?

00:09:41.774 --> 00:09:43.074
Did I hear the third read?

00:09:44.017 --> 00:09:45.524
- Oh, hold on, okay, let's go back.

00:09:45.524 --> 00:09:46.804
So the three reads.
- Let's go back.

00:09:46.804 --> 00:09:48.034
- So the first one, read the problem.

00:09:48.034 --> 00:09:51.034
And then I say, "Okay, what
is this problem about?"

00:09:51.034 --> 00:09:52.674
And then you're telling me

00:09:52.674 --> 00:09:55.244
what you think the problem is about.

00:09:55.244 --> 00:09:57.254
I'm not confirming or affirming.

00:09:57.254 --> 00:09:59.064
I just wanna know.

00:09:59.064 --> 00:10:01.031
The second read, now I ask you,

00:10:01.031 --> 00:10:03.724
"What are we trying to find out?"

00:10:03.724 --> 00:10:05.824
So a little different there.

00:10:05.824 --> 00:10:07.304
You know what, parents can use it, too.

00:10:07.304 --> 00:10:08.774
So, parents, I hope you're taking note.

00:10:08.774 --> 00:10:10.504
What are we trying to find out?

00:10:10.504 --> 00:10:12.574
And then we read the problem again,

00:10:12.574 --> 00:10:13.891
and now I'm gonna ask you,

00:10:13.891 --> 00:10:17.254
"What information might
be useful to solve this?"

00:10:17.254 --> 00:10:18.514
So in knowing information,

00:10:18.514 --> 00:10:21.414
now we're starting to get at
what the math is behind that.

00:10:22.674 --> 00:10:25.454
- That makes sense, yes, yes.

00:10:25.454 --> 00:10:26.534
Man, that sounds like the teacher

00:10:26.534 --> 00:10:27.884
would get a lot of information

00:10:27.884 --> 00:10:31.254
about what the student
knows and what they can do

00:10:31.254 --> 00:10:34.254
and all of those from
that kind of interaction.

00:10:34.254 --> 00:10:35.094
Great.
- Definitely.

00:10:35.094 --> 00:10:37.094
I think the intent is to really get behind

00:10:37.094 --> 00:10:38.044
what students are thinking.

00:10:38.044 --> 00:10:39.471
How are you thinking about the math?

00:10:39.471 --> 00:10:42.017
And that helps me to understand
what you know about the math

00:10:42.017 --> 00:10:44.304
and what misconceptions you might have.

00:10:44.304 --> 00:10:45.954
- Yeah, yeah.

00:10:45.954 --> 00:10:49.854
What do you see as the biggest
challenges teachers face

00:10:49.854 --> 00:10:50.844
in teaching math,

00:10:50.844 --> 00:10:53.534
and maybe it's different
for elementary students

00:10:53.534 --> 00:10:56.064
versus teachers of older students,

00:10:56.064 --> 00:10:58.457
but just what are some
of the big challenges?

00:11:00.524 --> 00:11:02.784
- I think the challenge that we face

00:11:02.784 --> 00:11:04.994
since like time immemorial

00:11:04.994 --> 00:11:08.834
is it's been illuminated and
heightened during the pandemic,

00:11:08.834 --> 00:11:11.634
And that's the challenge
of how do I meet the needs

00:11:11.634 --> 00:11:15.434
of so many different levels
of readiness in my classroom?

00:11:15.434 --> 00:11:17.704
If I teach seventh grade math,

00:11:17.704 --> 00:11:21.354
but I have students who
are academically ready

00:11:21.354 --> 00:11:23.864
for content maybe at
the fifth grade level,

00:11:23.864 --> 00:11:25.854
or I have students who might be ready

00:11:25.854 --> 00:11:28.234
to be challenged a little
more at the ninth grade level,

00:11:28.234 --> 00:11:30.094
as a seventh grade math teacher,

00:11:30.094 --> 00:11:32.184
the challenge for me
then becomes making sure

00:11:32.184 --> 00:11:33.384
I have a good depth and breadth

00:11:33.384 --> 00:11:35.274
of understanding of the content

00:11:35.274 --> 00:11:37.934
and that I can really
scaffold my instruction

00:11:37.934 --> 00:11:39.664
for my students, no matter where they are.

00:11:39.664 --> 00:11:41.954
That is a huge challenge
that was heightened

00:11:41.954 --> 00:11:43.084
during the pandemic.

00:11:43.084 --> 00:11:45.414
And when I say scaffolding,

00:11:45.414 --> 00:11:47.164
that's literally thinking

00:11:47.164 --> 00:11:49.754
about how you might build structures

00:11:49.754 --> 00:11:54.379
to help students grow from
where they are in the learning.

00:11:54.379 --> 00:11:55.807
Does that make sense, Kristen?

00:11:57.594 --> 00:11:59.334
- Yeah, it does, it does.

00:11:59.334 --> 00:12:01.034
And so now if we have students

00:12:01.034 --> 00:12:04.534
where there's even more variability

00:12:04.534 --> 00:12:07.014
throughout the class
of where students are,

00:12:07.014 --> 00:12:09.174
we've got even more levels

00:12:09.174 --> 00:12:12.444
that teachers are trying to address.

00:12:12.444 --> 00:12:14.114
What are some of the things they can do

00:12:14.114 --> 00:12:17.374
if they have students who
are at seven different levels

00:12:17.374 --> 00:12:20.757
in what they're trying to learn?

00:12:22.584 --> 00:12:26.244
- I think we want to
first encourage teachers

00:12:26.244 --> 00:12:29.144
to stay grounded in what the expectation

00:12:29.144 --> 00:12:30.704
and the standard is for the students.

00:12:30.704 --> 00:12:31.664
Because if you immediately,

00:12:31.664 --> 00:12:32.974
and I'm only saying this from a teacher

00:12:32.974 --> 00:12:35.093
who has the experience.

00:12:35.093 --> 00:12:37.544
I've taught in a classroom
where you have students

00:12:37.544 --> 00:12:42.544
who their literacy level is
pretty much non-existent,

00:12:42.814 --> 00:12:45.724
versus students who just
needs to be challenged

00:12:45.724 --> 00:12:47.284
and sent to higher level math.

00:12:47.284 --> 00:12:48.644
I have been there, okay,

00:12:48.644 --> 00:12:53.594
so I understand the immediate reactions

00:12:53.594 --> 00:12:55.474
to first go to like
the reality of the fact

00:12:55.474 --> 00:12:57.174
that I have to cover so much content

00:12:57.174 --> 00:12:58.374
with all of these students.

00:12:58.374 --> 00:12:59.784
And I think if we start at that point,

00:12:59.784 --> 00:13:01.074
it feels too overwhelming.

00:13:01.074 --> 00:13:02.834
It can feel pretty daunting.

00:13:02.834 --> 00:13:04.144
That's a daunting task.

00:13:04.144 --> 00:13:05.094
So I think it's important

00:13:05.094 --> 00:13:08.574
for our teachers to really get clear on

00:13:08.574 --> 00:13:11.454
what is it that my students need to know,

00:13:11.454 --> 00:13:13.214
what's the grade level standard,

00:13:13.214 --> 00:13:14.384
and what are the expectations

00:13:14.384 --> 00:13:18.227
that I personally hold for my students?

00:13:19.614 --> 00:13:21.804
I had just mentioned the interrupted

00:13:21.804 --> 00:13:23.854
and unfinished learning.

00:13:23.854 --> 00:13:26.114
Sometimes, Kristen, when we have a lot

00:13:26.114 --> 00:13:29.544
of interrupted learning,
it can lead to assumptions.

00:13:29.544 --> 00:13:33.254
Meaning I can very easily assume

00:13:33.254 --> 00:13:37.544
that my students don't
know A, B, or C content

00:13:37.544 --> 00:13:40.514
simply because of the fact
that they missed so much.

00:13:40.514 --> 00:13:43.817
And I think when we have
assumptions like that,

00:13:43.817 --> 00:13:45.574
I have a colleague, Chase Nordengren,

00:13:45.574 --> 00:13:49.694
and he always says that
assumptions are a threat to equity.

00:13:49.694 --> 00:13:51.004
And I think in this case,

00:13:51.004 --> 00:13:52.884
assumptions could be a threat to equity

00:13:52.884 --> 00:13:55.204
because then it could
start to limit the content

00:13:55.204 --> 00:13:57.084
that our students are exposed to.

00:13:57.084 --> 00:13:58.744
And ultimately what we wanna do

00:13:58.744 --> 00:14:00.924
is make sure that we're
exposing our students

00:14:00.924 --> 00:14:03.904
to grade level content

00:14:03.904 --> 00:14:07.354
irregardless of where their
readiness level might be.

00:14:07.354 --> 00:14:10.714
And the challenge then for
teachers is how to do that.

00:14:10.714 --> 00:14:12.087
That's the biggest challenge.

00:14:12.087 --> 00:14:13.884
That was a challenge I faced
throughout my entire career,

00:14:13.884 --> 00:14:15.544
is helping people understand,

00:14:15.544 --> 00:14:18.264
how do I keep my students engaged

00:14:18.264 --> 00:14:20.764
at levels that honor where they are,

00:14:20.764 --> 00:14:22.864
but still hold the standard or expectation

00:14:22.864 --> 00:14:24.204
for their grade level?

00:14:24.204 --> 00:14:27.544
And I think that is a matter

00:14:27.544 --> 00:14:28.924
of really getting clear on the content.

00:14:28.924 --> 00:14:31.137
So let's break it, make it real.

00:14:32.244 --> 00:14:35.014
If I'm a student who
really is still struggling

00:14:35.014 --> 00:14:36.514
with my multiplication facts,

00:14:36.514 --> 00:14:38.084
for the life of me, I cannot tell you

00:14:38.084 --> 00:14:41.564
what seven times six is, that
trips me up every single time.

00:14:41.564 --> 00:14:43.044
That should not stop me

00:14:43.044 --> 00:14:46.154
from then being able to
learn about measurement

00:14:46.154 --> 00:14:47.994
in terms of area and volume.

00:14:47.994 --> 00:14:50.074
Now the job becomes for my teachers,

00:14:50.074 --> 00:14:53.564
how do I still teach
this higher level concept

00:14:54.404 --> 00:14:56.584
while still throwing in opportunities

00:14:56.584 --> 00:14:58.107
for me to practice my facts?

00:14:59.259 --> 00:15:02.014
That's a daily challenge
that teachers face,

00:15:02.014 --> 00:15:04.834
so we wanna make sure that
we still expose students

00:15:04.834 --> 00:15:08.234
to that higher level standard
that we want them to be at.

00:15:08.234 --> 00:15:09.924
Another challenge for teachers then

00:15:09.924 --> 00:15:14.924
is to really understand their
content across grade levels.

00:15:15.554 --> 00:15:18.294
So we think about, yes, if
I teach seventh grade math,

00:15:18.294 --> 00:15:21.374
I have to be a master at that,

00:15:21.374 --> 00:15:23.314
but I also need to understand

00:15:23.314 --> 00:15:24.874
third, fourth, and fifth grade math,

00:15:24.874 --> 00:15:26.754
and sixth grade, and then eighth, ninth,

00:15:26.754 --> 00:15:28.234
and 10th grade math,

00:15:28.234 --> 00:15:31.864
because there are some
concepts that you teach

00:15:31.864 --> 00:15:36.004
where you absolutely have to
know the prerequisite skills.

00:15:36.004 --> 00:15:37.064
You have to know them.

00:15:37.064 --> 00:15:38.774
And so now my job then as a teacher

00:15:38.774 --> 00:15:41.454
is to figure out how
do I weave that knowing

00:15:41.454 --> 00:15:43.294
into the lessons?

00:15:43.294 --> 00:15:44.744
Is that making sense, Kristen?

00:15:44.744 --> 00:15:46.384
- It absolutely does,

00:15:46.384 --> 00:15:51.384
and I think that point about
having both the exposure

00:15:52.174 --> 00:15:54.254
to grade level skills

00:15:54.254 --> 00:15:59.074
and the ability to start to
practice those prerequisites

00:15:59.074 --> 00:16:01.634
that are really necessary to be successful

00:16:01.634 --> 00:16:05.274
on those grade level skills
is such a difficult balance.

00:16:05.274 --> 00:16:08.884
There's only so many
hours in a day (laughing)

00:16:08.884 --> 00:16:09.717
of where students get.

00:16:09.717 --> 00:16:11.944
And for a teacher, I can imagine,

00:16:11.944 --> 00:16:15.104
do teachers need to basically prepare

00:16:15.104 --> 00:16:17.024
lots of different lessons?

00:16:17.024 --> 00:16:21.314
Or how do they think about
preparing say that area unit?

00:16:21.314 --> 00:16:22.594
And they know they have some students

00:16:22.594 --> 00:16:24.284
that struggle with multiplication,

00:16:24.284 --> 00:16:25.354
but they have some students

00:16:25.354 --> 00:16:29.714
who maybe already understand
volume and increase.

00:16:29.714 --> 00:16:33.404
How do they go about just the preparation

00:16:33.404 --> 00:16:37.027
for teaching that area standard?

00:16:38.654 --> 00:16:40.801
- I feel overwhelmed just
hearing that question,

00:16:40.801 --> 00:16:41.634
because if I were a teacher

00:16:41.634 --> 00:16:43.924
and thought that I had to then plan

00:16:43.924 --> 00:16:45.844
a bunch of different lessons

00:16:45.844 --> 00:16:47.574
to meet the needs of all of my students,

00:16:47.574 --> 00:16:50.804
I would probably decide
that, you know what,

00:16:50.804 --> 00:16:52.404
I probably need a break from teaching.

00:16:52.404 --> 00:16:54.764
We don't wanna take that perspective

00:16:54.764 --> 00:16:57.494
of needing to design lessons

00:16:57.494 --> 00:16:59.444
that meet the needs of
every single student

00:16:59.444 --> 00:17:00.964
in the sense that I'm designing a lesson

00:17:00.964 --> 00:17:03.014
for each student group in my class.

00:17:03.014 --> 00:17:04.534
That's not the approach we wanna take.

00:17:04.534 --> 00:17:08.994
What we wanna do is really
make sure we understand

00:17:08.994 --> 00:17:11.694
what the big lesson is
that we're teaching,

00:17:11.694 --> 00:17:15.234
and in our lesson design,
build in opportunities

00:17:15.234 --> 00:17:17.414
to really have student discourse.

00:17:17.414 --> 00:17:19.137
We have to have students talking,

00:17:19.984 --> 00:17:21.094
telling how they're thinking.

00:17:21.094 --> 00:17:22.194
And the reason you're doing that

00:17:22.194 --> 00:17:24.004
is because you're leveraging

00:17:24.004 --> 00:17:27.534
your students' funds of
knowledge in the classroom.

00:17:27.534 --> 00:17:29.714
You're helping them to support

00:17:29.714 --> 00:17:30.974
the instruction of this lesson.

00:17:30.974 --> 00:17:34.694
So, what does that mean in layman's terms?

00:17:34.694 --> 00:17:37.384
If I have a student in my classroom

00:17:37.384 --> 00:17:39.204
who's really struggling
with those basic facts

00:17:39.204 --> 00:17:42.184
and that's keeping them from understanding

00:17:42.184 --> 00:17:44.094
how we're doing the Pythagorean theorem

00:17:44.094 --> 00:17:45.104
and how we're drawing that out,

00:17:45.104 --> 00:17:47.197
using triangles to figure it out,

00:17:48.184 --> 00:17:51.934
what I wanna do then is make
sure I'm asking questions

00:17:51.934 --> 00:17:55.174
that gets that student talking
about what they understand

00:17:55.174 --> 00:17:57.374
and then possibly showing their work

00:17:57.374 --> 00:17:58.794
and how they're thinking.

00:17:58.794 --> 00:18:01.284
And then as a teacher, this
gives me an opportunity now

00:18:01.284 --> 00:18:04.014
to maybe take how my students are thinking

00:18:04.014 --> 00:18:06.124
and start to tell a story, right?

00:18:06.124 --> 00:18:09.464
So, if I'm engaging in a lesson

00:18:09.464 --> 00:18:11.924
and I know that I'm gonna
have a group of students

00:18:11.924 --> 00:18:13.624
who are gonna get it right away,

00:18:13.624 --> 00:18:17.274
I'm not gonna start off by
using their work as an exemplar.

00:18:17.274 --> 00:18:20.444
I wanna tell a story
that builds on the math,

00:18:20.444 --> 00:18:25.444
such that I'm starting with
the student who is missing,

00:18:25.764 --> 00:18:28.174
they might have greater
misconceptions, right?

00:18:28.174 --> 00:18:29.484
And then I'm gonna build on that

00:18:29.484 --> 00:18:32.654
and I'm gonna draw in
another student's sample work

00:18:32.654 --> 00:18:35.264
where they have a greater understanding.

00:18:35.264 --> 00:18:37.174
And in building that story,

00:18:37.174 --> 00:18:39.844
then students can start to
have a better understanding

00:18:39.844 --> 00:18:41.124
of the concepts.

00:18:41.124 --> 00:18:44.174
So that's a very long-winded way of saying

00:18:44.174 --> 00:18:46.514
we're not designing lessons.

00:18:46.514 --> 00:18:48.034
You're not taking one lesson

00:18:48.034 --> 00:18:50.974
and designing like 30 other
mini lessons to go with it.

00:18:50.974 --> 00:18:53.694
What you're doing is
building opportunities

00:18:53.694 --> 00:18:55.544
in the lesson that you're designing

00:18:55.544 --> 00:18:57.704
to uncover what students know,

00:18:57.704 --> 00:18:59.794
to give everybody an
opportunity to engage,

00:18:59.794 --> 00:19:01.244
no matter what they know, right?

00:19:01.244 --> 00:19:05.884
So if you are just at the level,

00:19:05.884 --> 00:19:09.544
the basic level on this
ladder of instruction

00:19:09.544 --> 00:19:10.584
that we're climbing,

00:19:10.584 --> 00:19:12.244
then I wanna make sure you have a voice

00:19:12.244 --> 00:19:14.004
in this discussion in this lesson.

00:19:14.004 --> 00:19:16.174
So I'm building in equitable opportunities

00:19:16.174 --> 00:19:17.544
for everyone to participate.

00:19:17.544 --> 00:19:20.964
And in doing that, ultimately,
I'm gonna meet my goal

00:19:20.964 --> 00:19:23.674
of making sure that all
students are getting access

00:19:23.674 --> 00:19:27.137
to that top rung and they're
moving up the ladder.

00:19:28.314 --> 00:19:30.344
- That makes sense, that makes sense.

00:19:30.344 --> 00:19:33.824
So, both of us work for organizations

00:19:33.824 --> 00:19:38.704
that believe that technology
can potentially have a role

00:19:38.704 --> 00:19:41.154
in helping to address
this particular problem

00:19:41.154 --> 00:19:43.174
of thinking about differentiation

00:19:43.174 --> 00:19:46.004
and making sure that students
get the support they need

00:19:46.004 --> 00:19:48.724
and access to grade level material.

00:19:48.724 --> 00:19:52.424
And we're really happy
with our partnership

00:19:52.424 --> 00:19:54.724
that's created something
called MAP Accelerator

00:19:54.724 --> 00:19:57.834
to help teachers meet some of those needs.

00:19:57.834 --> 00:19:59.874
Can you talk a little bit about

00:20:01.074 --> 00:20:03.264
that MAP Accelerator experience

00:20:03.264 --> 00:20:04.847
and how it might fit into this?

00:20:05.724 --> 00:20:06.557
- Definitely.

00:20:06.557 --> 00:20:08.854
I'm really glad that you
mentioned the MAP Accelerator.

00:20:08.854 --> 00:20:12.824
So, Khan academy has partnered with NWEA

00:20:12.824 --> 00:20:16.314
and we've partnered to
create MAP Accelerator.

00:20:16.314 --> 00:20:19.774
The reason behind the
partnership is one, okay,

00:20:19.774 --> 00:20:23.924
Khan Academy by itself is a
dynamic and wonderful tool.

00:20:23.924 --> 00:20:28.924
When you bring in MAP Growth,
which is our assessment,

00:20:28.954 --> 00:20:32.344
MAP Growth is a test that students take,

00:20:32.344 --> 00:20:34.414
and the data, their results,

00:20:34.414 --> 00:20:37.004
tell us what they're ready to learn.

00:20:37.004 --> 00:20:39.554
It tells us what their
level of readiness is.

00:20:39.554 --> 00:20:41.124
So, it's not a test of mastery.

00:20:41.124 --> 00:20:42.934
It's not saying, all
right, I'm gonna test you

00:20:42.934 --> 00:20:45.064
on all the fifth grade skills to see,

00:20:45.064 --> 00:20:46.564
have you mastered those?

00:20:46.564 --> 00:20:47.524
It's gonna say, you know what,

00:20:47.524 --> 00:20:50.534
I'm gonna test you on
a plethora of skills,

00:20:50.534 --> 00:20:53.664
and the test is gonna adjust
based on how you answer,

00:20:53.664 --> 00:20:55.924
because what I'm trying
to do is figure out,

00:20:55.924 --> 00:20:57.954
what's your zone for learning?

00:20:57.954 --> 00:21:00.034
What's your zone of proximal development?

00:21:00.034 --> 00:21:02.374
And then once I have that information,

00:21:02.374 --> 00:21:05.304
it's put into MAP Accelerator,

00:21:05.304 --> 00:21:07.874
and then MAP Accelerator designs

00:21:07.874 --> 00:21:11.174
or it creates a learning
path for me as a student

00:21:11.174 --> 00:21:12.904
that grows with me.

00:21:12.904 --> 00:21:15.394
So it starts at my zone,

00:21:15.394 --> 00:21:18.744
and as I progress through
my zone and I learn more,

00:21:18.744 --> 00:21:19.644
it moves with me.

00:21:19.644 --> 00:21:22.444
So it's just a wonderful,
powerful tool that we have

00:21:22.444 --> 00:21:24.554
to really support teachers

00:21:24.554 --> 00:21:27.504
and the wide variety of student
readiness in our classroom.

00:21:28.974 --> 00:21:31.034
- So we've got teachers
doing all the great things

00:21:31.034 --> 00:21:33.590
that you described with those activities

00:21:33.590 --> 00:21:36.804
that are accessible for
students who have skill gaps,

00:21:36.804 --> 00:21:39.654
but also challenge other
students and all of that.

00:21:39.654 --> 00:21:42.054
And then paired with
that, we have this tool

00:21:42.054 --> 00:21:45.064
that when students are
working individually

00:21:45.064 --> 00:21:48.494
can help really target where they are

00:21:48.494 --> 00:21:51.494
based on those MAP Growth scores

00:21:51.494 --> 00:21:53.564
and help bring those together.

00:21:53.564 --> 00:21:56.244
So, that seems like a good balance.

00:21:56.244 --> 00:22:01.244
How do you see technology
fitting into classrooms?

00:22:02.984 --> 00:22:04.204
- That is the key question.

00:22:04.204 --> 00:22:06.114
Now we have to put this
all in context, Kristen.

00:22:06.114 --> 00:22:08.574
We have to keep in mind that
our students have engaged

00:22:08.574 --> 00:22:10.654
with technology over
the past couple of years

00:22:10.654 --> 00:22:12.604
in a way that was very different

00:22:12.604 --> 00:22:14.894
than how they engaged before, right?

00:22:14.894 --> 00:22:18.137
So, we have to be mindful of
the fact that our students,

00:22:19.799 --> 00:22:21.104
they feel some type of
way about technology.

00:22:21.104 --> 00:22:23.014
They could feel overwhelmed.

00:22:23.014 --> 00:22:24.774
They could have gotten
into certain routines.

00:22:24.774 --> 00:22:27.774
So what we wanna do is really
empower teachers and students

00:22:27.774 --> 00:22:31.564
to have the routines that you wanna employ

00:22:31.564 --> 00:22:32.504
from MAP Accelerator.

00:22:32.504 --> 00:22:35.304
A routine might be, we've
got 30 minutes a week,

00:22:35.304 --> 00:22:36.834
that's our MAP Accelerator time.

00:22:36.834 --> 00:22:38.364
That's my Khan Academy time.

00:22:38.364 --> 00:22:41.854
And then let's create some
rituals to support that.

00:22:41.854 --> 00:22:45.684
And the ritual might be something
like a Talk It Out Tuesday

00:22:45.684 --> 00:22:49.104
or a Work It Out Wednesday,
or a Think About It Thursday.

00:22:49.104 --> 00:22:51.474
So what we wanna do is
create these rituals

00:22:51.474 --> 00:22:53.664
that engage students
and teachers together.

00:22:53.664 --> 00:22:56.004
So, we've got the
technology in the center,

00:22:56.004 --> 00:22:58.914
but we wanna use our
discourse to bring us together

00:22:58.914 --> 00:23:01.844
so that it becomes an
interactive tool for everybody.

00:23:01.844 --> 00:23:02.921
So a teacher might say,

00:23:02.921 --> 00:23:04.674
"All right, it's Talk It Out Tuesday.

00:23:04.674 --> 00:23:07.064
What I want you to do after
your MAP Accelerator time

00:23:07.064 --> 00:23:10.504
is go back and find that one problem

00:23:10.504 --> 00:23:11.984
that you kept getting stuck doing,

00:23:11.984 --> 00:23:15.234
you kept having to hit
the hint function for.

00:23:15.234 --> 00:23:18.854
The problem just, hmm, you
had a little struggle with it,

00:23:18.854 --> 00:23:19.914
productive struggle.

00:23:19.914 --> 00:23:21.784
And what I want you to
do is now talk it out

00:23:21.784 --> 00:23:23.414
with a partner in the classroom.

00:23:23.414 --> 00:23:24.624
And in the talking it out,

00:23:24.624 --> 00:23:26.644
you might gain a little bit more clarity."

00:23:26.644 --> 00:23:28.964
Or we could have a Work It Out Wednesday,

00:23:28.964 --> 00:23:33.424
where students are encouraged
to go to their caregivers

00:23:33.424 --> 00:23:35.704
and show and tell.

00:23:35.704 --> 00:23:39.664
Let them see a MAP Accelerator experience

00:23:39.664 --> 00:23:41.734
and then work it out with that person.

00:23:41.734 --> 00:23:43.334
So that gives the student opportunity

00:23:43.334 --> 00:23:45.144
to then step outside of their head,

00:23:45.144 --> 00:23:46.404
do their thinking out loud,

00:23:46.404 --> 00:23:48.174
and continue working on their path.

00:23:48.174 --> 00:23:50.184
So I think, Kristen, what we wanna do

00:23:50.184 --> 00:23:51.894
is leverage technology in a way

00:23:51.894 --> 00:23:55.054
that's really gonna empower
our students right now,

00:23:55.054 --> 00:23:57.744
really being mindful about
the experiences they've had

00:23:57.744 --> 00:24:01.217
these past couple of years,
and how do we re-empower them.

00:24:03.084 --> 00:24:04.104
- I heard a couple of things.

00:24:04.104 --> 00:24:08.844
I heard the peer-to-peer
learning piece going on

00:24:08.844 --> 00:24:11.204
and we know that that's powerful.

00:24:11.204 --> 00:24:15.144
And then I heard them
explaining to a caregiver

00:24:15.144 --> 00:24:17.584
which then puts them where it goes back

00:24:17.584 --> 00:24:18.944
to what we were talking
about at the beginning

00:24:18.944 --> 00:24:23.544
about using math language
with an adult maybe,

00:24:23.544 --> 00:24:27.244
and they can become the expert
and explain how it's done.

00:24:27.244 --> 00:24:28.684
I like both of those ideas

00:24:28.684 --> 00:24:32.364
where we're not just having a student

00:24:32.364 --> 00:24:35.814
sitting in front of a computer
isolated by themselves,

00:24:35.814 --> 00:24:38.857
but creating more of a
community around the technology.

00:24:39.884 --> 00:24:40.717
- That's right.

00:24:40.717 --> 00:24:42.914
We need to have so much more discourse.

00:24:42.914 --> 00:24:45.674
That's what's gonna
bring everything alive.

00:24:45.674 --> 00:24:47.804
- Yeah, and I feel like
after this past year,

00:24:47.804 --> 00:24:50.104
everyone is just really eager

00:24:50.104 --> 00:24:52.744
to make those personal
connections with people

00:24:52.744 --> 00:24:57.511
that were more difficult when
we were all in quarantine

00:24:58.734 --> 00:25:01.134
in the COVID situation.

00:25:01.134 --> 00:25:01.967
- That's right.

00:25:01.967 --> 00:25:06.864
- So, we talked a bit
about both the past year

00:25:07.704 --> 00:25:11.674
and how those students were fairing.

00:25:11.674 --> 00:25:13.714
One of the biggest questions we got was,

00:25:13.714 --> 00:25:15.824
how do we keep students motivated?

00:25:15.824 --> 00:25:18.774
How do we keep them engaged in learning

00:25:18.774 --> 00:25:19.947
and what they're doing?

00:25:22.054 --> 00:25:23.434
Are there technology tools

00:25:23.434 --> 00:25:25.204
that can help keep students engaged?

00:25:25.204 --> 00:25:28.024
And what other suggestions
do you have for teachers?

00:25:28.024 --> 00:25:29.874
- Yeah, well, one, MAP Accelerator.

00:25:29.874 --> 00:25:30.954
So we definitely wanna make sure

00:25:30.954 --> 00:25:33.374
that whatever digital
tools that we're using

00:25:33.374 --> 00:25:35.844
with our young people,
we're making it something

00:25:35.844 --> 00:25:38.804
so that it is a
self-regulated tool for them.

00:25:38.804 --> 00:25:41.564
But we have to find ways
to stay engaged with them

00:25:42.623 --> 00:25:44.584
and get regular feedback from them

00:25:44.584 --> 00:25:48.424
and allow them time to share
with us what they're doing.

00:25:48.424 --> 00:25:50.144
So, I think that's one
of the biggest tools

00:25:50.144 --> 00:25:52.314
that we need to employ.

00:25:52.314 --> 00:25:55.494
I would love to see
teachers supporting students

00:25:55.494 --> 00:25:57.434
in goal setting more,

00:25:57.434 --> 00:25:59.714
because we all like to experience success.

00:25:59.714 --> 00:26:00.814
It feels good.

00:26:00.814 --> 00:26:02.664
It's empowers us, right?

00:26:02.664 --> 00:26:04.634
It gives us inspiration and motivation

00:26:04.634 --> 00:26:05.954
to keep moving forward.

00:26:05.954 --> 00:26:08.534
And so, I would love to see teachers

00:26:08.534 --> 00:26:11.204
working alongside students to set goals

00:26:11.204 --> 00:26:13.444
with their MAP Accelerator,

00:26:13.444 --> 00:26:15.274
and then checking in on
those goals regularly,

00:26:15.274 --> 00:26:16.314
short-term goals, too.

00:26:16.314 --> 00:26:18.774
So, we're not talking about
by the end of the school year,

00:26:18.774 --> 00:26:20.114
I want you to be able to do this.

00:26:20.114 --> 00:26:22.474
No, I might set a weekly goal,

00:26:22.474 --> 00:26:24.894
because I have weekly
MAP Accelerator time,

00:26:24.894 --> 00:26:27.834
and that goal could be
around two or three skills.

00:26:27.834 --> 00:26:29.964
And I wanna make sure
that's an achievable goal,

00:26:29.964 --> 00:26:31.254
it feels achievable,

00:26:31.254 --> 00:26:34.544
and it's something that I
can track my own progress on

00:26:34.544 --> 00:26:37.544
and I can talk about it and I
can find reasons to celebrate.

00:26:38.414 --> 00:26:40.054
- Fenesha, you're speaking my language.

00:26:40.054 --> 00:26:41.834
I can't tell you how often (laughing)

00:26:41.834 --> 00:26:44.204
I talk about goals and goal setting,

00:26:44.204 --> 00:26:46.174
and that is a way to motivate,

00:26:46.174 --> 00:26:49.214
because, yeah, we are
motivated when we see success

00:26:49.214 --> 00:26:51.757
and when we feel like
we're gonna be successful.

00:26:53.794 --> 00:26:57.634
Nothing shows success like past success.

00:26:57.634 --> 00:27:00.114
So yeah, goal setting is huge for me,

00:27:00.114 --> 00:27:04.814
so I love that we're
connected on that. (chuckling)

00:27:04.814 --> 00:27:08.254
So, we're almost already out of time.

00:27:08.254 --> 00:27:10.484
We have a couple of questions.

00:27:10.484 --> 00:27:11.734
We have a question from Facebook,

00:27:11.734 --> 00:27:14.844
from Charles Drayden who has just,

00:27:14.844 --> 00:27:16.514
this might be a good one to end on.

00:27:16.514 --> 00:27:17.734
I have a young child.

00:27:17.734 --> 00:27:20.604
How can I help him flourish
in his math studies?

00:27:20.604 --> 00:27:22.344
And I like that question,

00:27:22.344 --> 00:27:26.789
that isn't just succeed,
but flourish. (chuckling)

00:27:26.789 --> 00:27:28.094
- I love it.
- So, any other tips you have

00:27:28.094 --> 00:27:29.974
that we haven't gotten to?

00:27:29.974 --> 00:27:31.614
- Well, thank you so
much for that question

00:27:31.614 --> 00:27:33.614
and thank you for chiming in on Facebook.

00:27:33.614 --> 00:27:36.694
I think it's really important
for parents and caregivers

00:27:36.694 --> 00:27:39.014
to find ways to bring math alive

00:27:39.014 --> 00:27:40.454
in our students' everyday life.

00:27:40.454 --> 00:27:41.287
And sometimes people say,

00:27:41.287 --> 00:27:44.934
"I don't even know how I use
math in my everyday life."

00:27:44.934 --> 00:27:46.664
Let me tell you one of my favorite books.

00:27:46.664 --> 00:27:48.564
It's a book called "Math Curse."

00:27:48.564 --> 00:27:50.434
It's a children's book
and it just talks about

00:27:50.434 --> 00:27:52.344
how everything is a math problem.

00:27:52.344 --> 00:27:53.834
So, a lot of our school students

00:27:53.834 --> 00:27:55.434
are starting back in school.

00:27:55.434 --> 00:27:57.134
You might have an eighth grader

00:27:57.134 --> 00:27:59.974
who doesn't have to wear a
uniform to school every day,

00:27:59.974 --> 00:28:02.354
and they have the opportunity
to express themselves.

00:28:02.354 --> 00:28:04.684
And you know at that age
it's all about them anyway.

00:28:04.684 --> 00:28:07.054
And so, how does that
turn into a math problem?

00:28:07.054 --> 00:28:09.014
You could ask them, "How
many different outfits

00:28:09.014 --> 00:28:10.184
could you put together

00:28:10.184 --> 00:28:13.124
from these four or five
items of clothing?"

00:28:13.124 --> 00:28:14.634
That's a math problem.

00:28:14.634 --> 00:28:16.024
You might have a second grader

00:28:16.024 --> 00:28:19.044
who's totally fascinated by the cicadas.

00:28:19.044 --> 00:28:21.756
Well, here in the Midwest,
at least we have cicadas.

00:28:21.756 --> 00:28:24.214
It's cicada time.
- I heard, I heard.

00:28:24.214 --> 00:28:27.024
- Yes, and at nighttime,
the sound is beautiful.

00:28:27.024 --> 00:28:29.804
And so, they might be
so fascinated by cicadas

00:28:29.804 --> 00:28:31.574
and grasshoppers and crickets.

00:28:31.574 --> 00:28:33.164
How does that turn into a math problem?

00:28:33.164 --> 00:28:35.004
I want you to go listen to those insects

00:28:35.004 --> 00:28:38.034
and I want you to tell me
who's chirping the fastest.

00:28:38.034 --> 00:28:39.654
So now we're talking
about frequency, right?

00:28:39.654 --> 00:28:41.234
So, everything becomes a math problem.

00:28:41.234 --> 00:28:44.034
So, Charles, what I think
you and other parents can do

00:28:44.034 --> 00:28:47.634
is really find ways to make
math come alive in everyday life

00:28:47.634 --> 00:28:50.194
because math is fun and it should be fun.

00:28:50.194 --> 00:28:53.854
And we have to all be able
to say that learning is fun.

00:28:53.854 --> 00:28:55.307
Let's not forget that.

00:28:56.794 --> 00:28:58.594
- That seems like a great
note to end this on.

00:28:58.594 --> 00:29:01.294
Thank you so much for joining me today.

00:29:01.294 --> 00:29:04.604
It was great to hear
your wisdom and ideas,

00:29:04.604 --> 00:29:07.374
and I love that you are out
there working with teachers

00:29:07.374 --> 00:29:10.154
to better support students for learning.

00:29:10.154 --> 00:29:11.504
- Thank you so much, Kristen.

00:29:11.504 --> 00:29:13.564
I always enjoy working with Khan Academy.

00:29:13.564 --> 00:29:15.494
Thank you for having me.

00:29:15.494 --> 00:29:16.327
- Excellent.

00:29:16.327 --> 00:29:18.484
Thank you all and we
will see you next time.

